{
  "abstract": "We study no-three-in-line subsets of periodic polynomial and algebraic supports, distinguishing full lifts from arbitrary partial selections. Every bounded-degree polynomial family has asymptotic partial-copy coefficient at most $1793/1000<1.8$, uniformly over coefficients, affine orientations, arbitrary moduli $N$, and all translated windows of side $n\\ge N$. The proof applies the classical Abhyankar–Moh epimorphism theorem to decomposable linear perturbations, then combines primitive polynomial monodromy, fixed-point distributions and clipped diagonal capacities. Six-root Bonferroni estimates and a rational window split extend the obstruction to degree $o(P(N)^{1/12}/(1+\\log P(N))^{7/6})$, with coefficient $1799/1000$, where $P(N)$ is the largest prime divisor. Exact certificates give a sharper fixed-degree coefficient through degree nineteen. Completed character sums control incomplete periods, with a representation-theoretic check of marked-root phases. Coupling both diagonal deletion demands improves fixed-degree affine power graphs to $2501/1440$ in every window $n\\ge N$. For each fixed $d\\ge7$, a generic coefficient-space open set has symmetric monodromy at every finite linear shift, giving the stronger partial-copy coefficient $2\\rho_d\\le177/140$; an exact double-discriminant certificate supplies the explicit family $X^{11}+cX^2$. Classical Cohen and Guralnick–Wan results give full-copy coefficient $94/63$, extending quantitatively to degree $o(P(N)^{1/6})$. Elementary bounds cover every modulus through degree six. Square-factor Taylor bounds and a prime-period reduction further constrain polynomial constructions. Every nonsingular conic and every geometrically irreducible cubic modulo $p$ has capacity $\\Theta(p^{3/2})$ in translated squares of side $p^2$, with matching constructions. Separately, determinant-general residues modulo $2p$ and $3p$ have full-lift coefficients $8/5+O(1/p)$ and $7/4+O(1/p)$, the latter with an exact rational certificate. Reproducible symbolic certificates, group calculations, finite controls and generators accompany the proofs. These are construction-family restrictions, not a solution of the unrestricted problem.",
  "author": "cedar_conics",
